    "narrative": "The core of our lineup today rests on a heavy concentration of hitters facing Eduardo Rodriguez, whose 4.68 xERA provides the primary engine for our daily ceiling. **Matt Chapman** is our premier play at a 69.85 efficiency score; he benefits from a rare combination of leadoff duty, a favorable BvP history (29 PA), and a slow delivery from the mound that creates an opportunistic environment for his baserunning profile. **Dylan Beavers** (65.54) also emerges as an elite start, utilizing a +16% park factor and a platoon advantage to climb into our top tier, while **Geraldo Perdomo** and **Taylor Ward** anchor our middle-order production with high-leverage lineup spots (#2) and strong matchup profiles.\n\nThe rotation decisions for the final outfield and middle infield spots were tight. **Lawrence Butler** earns a starting nod at 60.88, aided by a significant park boost, whereas **Jung Hoo Lee** (52.06) and **Drew Gilbert** (46.53) were penalized heavily by the platoon disadvantage against Rodriguez, sliding into the lower-to-marginal bracket despite their speed profiles. The most difficult bench decision involved our utility depth; while **Jonathan Aranda** and **Gleyber Torres** were considered, they could not overcome the efficiency gaps created by the park factors favoring **Dylan Beavers** and **Taylor Ward**. We continue to monitor the IL statuses of **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** and **Will Smith**, whose absence leaves a void in our core production that we are currently compensating for with high-volume, opportunistic starts.\n\nRisks remain elevated across the board due to \"Lineup Pending\" designations for **Cal Raleigh**, **Gavin Sheets**, **Geraldo Perdomo**, **Luis Arraez**, **Matt Chapman**, **Drew Gilbert**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, and **Lawrence Butler**. Should these players shift toward the bottom third of their respective orders, their scores will regress significantly. Furthermore, while we are targeting the inefficiencies of Rodriguez and Burke, S, we must remain wary of the weather and defensive shifts that could stifle the production of our contact-heavy hitters like **Luis Arraez**. Owners should keep a close eye on final lineup cards to ensure our speed-dependent threats like **Masyn Winn** or **Brendan Donovan**\u2014if inserted late\u2014are maximized.",
